#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135160 :tutorial/content "Consider a two-dimensional vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ defined over a region $D.$ \n\nRecall that Green's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across the boundary of $D.$ \n\nWe can express Green's theorem in flux form as a single picture, shown below.\n\n![](images/52/52191ef5fea6e08b885dfd9ffea547e3843b8113847b532f8b4aa5d966d15213.png)\n\nWe'll now state the higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. Please note that we will restrict our attention to so-called **simple regions** for the remainder of this lesson. Simple regions in $\\mathbb R^3$ can be classified as type I, type II, *and* type III." :tutorial/id #uuid "6855af2b-f345-5f4d-aae2-49d6ee758d9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7606 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135164 :tutorial/content "Consider the vector field \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 2x+1,\\: 3z,\\: xy \\rangle\n$$\n\nand the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown below. \n\n![](images/cf/cf90197ac99a066db75154200de4cbee2494fef15deed6be7b7c4cd501ae8f26.png)\n\nLet's find the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$\n\nTo find the total flux directly, we would need to find the flux through each of the six surfaces of our rectangular solid and then add the results. However, using the divergence theorem\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n= \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V,\n$$\n\nwe can reduce this computation to a single triple integral.\n\nComputing the divergence of $\\mathbf{F},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =2+0+0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux through $S$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e318cc2d-81e3-5cbd-b0ce-2f076bfd06b8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7732 :tutorial/title "Calculating the Flux Across a Surface"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135170 :tutorial/content "The **divergence theorem** is a higher-dimensional analog of Green's theorem in flux form. It states the following:\n\n> *Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf{F} \\: \\textrm{d}V\n> =\n> \\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n> =\n> \\iint\\limits_{S} \\mathbf{F}\\cdot\\mathbf{n}\\:\\mathrm{d}S\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *where $\\mathbf{n}$ is the outward-pointing unit normal to $S.$*\n\nIn other words, the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf{F}$ inside $R$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ through the boundary of $R.$ \n\nThe divergence theorem can be summarized in a single diagram.\n\n![](images/ff/ffabcceb5f4fd520a57f909c476b267f584ac4dc3fb948a5c1996b71dbc74e6f.png)\n\nThe divergence theorem can be used to simplify flux calculations. Calculating the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across a surface can be time-consuming because we often need to consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across multiple faces that make up $S.$ The divergence theorem often allows us to reduce a complicated flux calculation to a single triple integral." :tutorial/id #uuid "ca1c4682-71c7-5950-950a-a33ad1e84c23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7773 :tutorial/title "The Divergence Theorem"}]}
